VR is often promoted as the ultimate medium in its capacity to imitate or indeed replace reality. In this respect VR is implicitly or explicitly compared to film. In particular,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid8018889  }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9911246\charrsid4279564 VR\rquote 
s goal of photorealism makes it clear how those who develop digital technologies and applications are influenced by the aesthetics and cultural role of mainstream film in the twentieth century. In the quest for photorealism, computer scientists and CG 
artists refer to the task of crossing the \'93uncanny valley,\'94 a term coined by ro
boticist Masahiro Mori. The claim is that as CG strives to create increasingly realistic 3D humans and animals, the figures become less rather than more sympathetic. The effect is eerie or uncanny, because the viewer becomes increasingly aware of the gap 
between the figures and authentic living beings. We propose to examine the uncanny valley in CG in the context of the history of the uncanny doubles in film. We argue that there is a clear continuity between this tradition and the uncanny doubles in digit
al media, such as avatars in VR.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid8018889  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9911246\charrsid4279564 As a medium that depends entirely on CG for its visual expression,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 
\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid8018889  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9911246\charrsid4279564 
VR shares with or indeed inherits from film an ambivalence about its status as a medium. Both VR and mainstream film are technologies of illusion and yet both claim in some sense to reproduce or appropriate reality. The uncanny oscillation between illusio
n and reality is a key feature of the aesthetics of both film and digital media.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\af1\insrsid11278685\charrsid10629126 
