\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af41 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid9911246 \hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 
Virtual reality (VR) is often promoted as the ultimate medium in its capacity to reproduce or indeed replace reality. The representational techniques underlying VR as a reality medium are those of 3D computer graphics (3D CG). In the quest for photorealis
\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 m, computer graphics artists and even some computer scientists refer to the task of crossing the }{\ltrch\fcs1 \af0\alang1025 \rtlch\fcs0 \f88\fs24\lang1025\langnp1025\insrsid9911246 \'93}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af41 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\lang1043\langfe1033\langnp1043\insrsid9911246 \hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 uncanny valley,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af41 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid9911246 \loch\af0\dbch\af41\hich\f0 \'94\loch\f0 \hich\f0 
 a term coined in the 1970s by a roboticist, Masahiro Mori (Mori et al., 2012 [1970]). Mori illustrated his concept with a graph in which \'93\loch\f0 huma\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 \hich\f0 n likeness\'94\loch\f0 \hich\f0 
 is plotted on the x-axis and what was translated as \'93\loch\f0 \hich\f0 affinity\'94\loch\f0 
 on the y-axis. He proposed that as robots become more human-like, our affinity for them increases, but only up to a point. Robots or artifacts (e.g. a prosthetic hand) that bear a \hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 \hich\f0 
close but not perfect resemblance to humans instead evoke a feeling of unease or uncanniness. Affinity plummets. As a robot or artifact achieves something closer to perfect realism, it moves back up the affinity curve. There is then an \'93\loch\f0 
\hich\f0 uncanny valley\'94\loch\f0  th\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 at\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 
 roboticists could avoid either by staying in the realm of the not-very-human or by achieving a perfect resemblance to the human.  Computer graphics borrowed the term from robotics, because realistic, but imperfect 3D CG humans and animals too can become 
\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 less rather than more sympathetic. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid11278685 
