The problem of the uncanny valley has been a concern for computer science and popular computer graphic art for decades. The computer science literature treats it as a psychological phenomenon relevant to human-computer interaction (Diehl et al., 2021; K
\'e4\loch\f0 t\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 syri et al, 2015). The CG artists\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 
 community regards the uncanny valley as a practical problem. Although they do not necessarily acknowledge it, both artists and researchers understand the phenomenon of the uncanny valley in terms of the earlier media of \hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 ph
\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 otography and film. The far side of the valley would be realistic photography and live-action film. This is at least ostensibly different from robotics, where Mori}{\ltrch\fcs1 \af0\alang1025 \rtlch\fcs0 
\f88\fs24\lang1025\langnp1025\insrsid9911246 \rquote }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af41 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid9911246 \hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 
s term originated, because robots are physical and operate in the physical world, alongside humans. In theory, the uncanny valley could describe the feeling of the uncanny produced by any 3D representation; a 3D landscape could be uncanny in this sense, i
\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 n which case one could actually have an uncanny 3D valley. But the uncanny valley problem is \hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 
almost always posed in the context of rendering human-like figures (and animals). It arises, then, that the challenge of CG is to recreate the human, or in other words, to make a human double. This is an ambition that CG shares with robotics. }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid11278685 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af41 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid9911246 \hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 
Our focus in this paper will be on the uncanny valley as a question of aesthetics, not human-computer interaction or perceptual psychology. We explore the aesthetic assumptions and representational practices of CG artists and popular audiences because it 
\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 \hich\f0 is these assumptions and practices that connect CG (and its application in screen-based and headset VR) to the realistic traditions of \'93\loch\f0 \hich\f0 mainstream\'94\loch\f0 
 cinema. We acknowledge that photorealism is not the whole story of CG and VR today, any more than the reali\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 sm\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 
 is the whole story of film. Many videogames and other digital experiences offer their players or viewers cartoon-like worlds that remain safely on the near side of the uncanny valley. There is also an important tradition of digital art using nonrealistic
\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 \hich\f0  computer graphics that we do not consider here. However, the allure of the photorealistic and the \'93\loch\f0 \hich\f0 real\'94\loch\f0 
 is still an important driver behind both technological and content development for VR experiences.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid11278685 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af41 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid9911246 \hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 
Specifically, we propose to examine the uncanny valley in CG in the context of the history of the uncanny and uncanny doubles in film. We begin with a look at the filmic tradition of the uncanny double. We then suggest that there is a clear continuity bet
\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 
ween this tradition with the uncanny doubles in digital media, such as avatars in VR and more recently AI generated imagery and even chatbots. We argue that VR shares with or indeed inherits from film an ambivalence about its status as a medium. Both VR a
\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 nd\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 
 mainstream film are technologies of illusion and yet both claim in some sense to reproduce or appropriate reality.  The uncanny oscillation between illusion and reality is a key feature of the aesthetics of both film and digital media.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid11278685 
