We have not addressed a defining element of the experience of the mirror worlds of VR: the way in which embodiment is staged. VR is immersive in a way different from the immersion of cinema and engages the kinesthetic and proprioceptive senses in a diffe
rent way, as many theorists, including contributors to this volume (e.g. Ceuterick; Chan), have noted. While, in this essay, we are focusing on the visual dimension of VR, grounded in computer graphics, we do not mean to underestimate the techniques and t
echnologies that are coordinated with computer graphics and make possible VR immersion and kinesthetic responses possible. VR is not the first medium to stage a kind of embodiment; the argument has long been made that the experience of }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af41 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid9911246 \hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 
cinema is embodied too. It is rather that VR embodiment is different in that the scene is adjusted in real time in response to changes in orientation and position, and this technical difference suggests a new kind of immersive spectatorship. The point we 
\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 wish to emphasize is th\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 
at this embodiment does not raise VR beyond the realm of mediation. (Despite the utopian claims of some enthusiasts, VR is still a representational medium in the tradition of photography and film.) Rather it opens a new gap parallel to the visual gap that
\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0  we have been exploring\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 \hich\f0 
this one between a particular mediation of embodied vision and the embodiment that we experience when we take off the headset. Ceuterick articulates this gap when she notes that VR is always an \'93\loch\f0 oscillation between content, the dev
\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 ic\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 \hich\f0 e and a potentially distracting physical environment forms an integral part of what virtual reality spectatorship is.\'94\loch\f0 
 In other words, VR embodiment fashions its own uncanny valley, or we could say that the uncanny valley of VR embodied spectatorship is m\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 
ultimodal. In either case, the La Ciotat effect is still operative. VR embodiment does not erase the uncanny quality of the experience; it adds another dimension to the uncanny. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid11278685 
