This essay, like some others in this collection, argues that CG and VR should be understood as belonging to and remediating the representational practices of live-action film. In his compelling essay opening this section of this volume, however, William U
\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 \hich\f0 ricchio suggests that VR is antithetical to cinema in its "visual constructions, organization of time, and possibilities of story.\'94\loch\f0  Uricchio\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 
s insights are valuable, and, we would argue, do not necessarily contradict the quality of CG and VR that most clo\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 se\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 
ly connects them to film: their ambivalent and uncanny claim to capture reality. CG and VR}{\ltrch\fcs1 \af0\alang1025 \rtlch\fcs0 \f88\fs24\lang1025\langnp1025\insrsid9911246 \rquote }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af41 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\insrsid9911246 
\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 s preoccupation with the uncanny valley indicates a similar concern to the one that has defined film as a medium\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 
the concern whether their medium is a mere substitution for the real and the authentic. Yet, film and VR each occupy or have occupied a differ\hich\af0\dbch\af41\loch\f0 ent position in the contemporary media landscape. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
